## 8. Known risks

### 8.1 Battery / GPU cost — HIGH

Setting `PAINT_WINDOW_WITH_TRANSFORMED_WINDOWS` forces KWin to **repaint the
entire screen every frame**. At camera framerate, on a laptop, this is the
largest technical risk in the project.

**Mitigations to explore in Phase 4:**
- Cap the effect at a lower framerate than the camera (e.g. 15–20 Hz) — tilt is
  slow; it does not need 30 Hz.
- Disable automatically on battery / low power profile.
- Only transform windows that are actually visible on the active output.
- Measure and **publish** the numbers. Users can make their own trade-off.

### 8.2 Default-ON camera on a public distro — ACKNOWLEDGED RISK

Default-on was chosen deliberately, in preference to a recommendation of
default-off. It is defensible **only if** the tray indicator, the no-network-code
guarantee, and the privacy documentation are airtight.

Expect this to be the first thing criticised in reviews and forum discussions.
Own it proactively in the documentation rather than being caught by it.

### 8.3 Naming — UNRESOLVED

See §11. **Blocks Phase 0.**

### 8.4 Hardware QA ceiling

We can test **two hardware profiles** (one desktop, one laptop). Driver quality
beyond that depends entirely on community bug reports. Plan beta recruitment from
early in Phase 2, not at 1.0.

### 8.5 Missing KDE packages

`artikulate, kamoso, kig, ktouch, rocs, umbrello` are unavailable in Trixie.
Users will notice and will file bugs. Document it up front.

### 8.6 Package-repository trust

Adding a third-party APT repo means our signing key can push arbitrary code as
root to every user who installed it. This is a serious responsibility:
- Protect the master key offline (§7.4).
- Publish a **transparency log** for the repo.
- Document key rotation and emergency revocation.
- Keep the number of custom packages **small** — most of the system should come
  from Debian, whose security team we are not competing with.

