## 10. Trademark obligations

### 10.1 The "Linux" mark — REQUIRED ACTION

Per the Linux Foundation's sublicensing programme:

> *"If you plan to market a Linux-based product or service to the public using a
> trademark that includes the element 'Linux'... you are required to apply for and
> obtain a sublicense from the Linux Foundation... It doesn't matter if your
> trademark is unregistered, or if you don't plan to make any money using the
> mark."*

- **Free**, perpetual, world-wide.
- **Required** whenever the name contains "Linux" as a brand identifier for
  software goods — which ours will.
- **Apply early** — it has lead time. ➜ Phase 0 task.
- The agreement includes an obligation **not to imply endorsement**.

### 10.2 Debian / KDE / upstream marks

- Redistribute freely under licence.
- **Do not** use Debian's or KDE's logos or trademarks in a way implying endorsement.
- Document Debian reuse and attribution in `/usr/share/doc`.
